#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172906} :activity/id #uuid "3b336b02-2871-5881-b0a6-2eb5ee14cff9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048489} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171844 :question/id #uuid "231de5e4-8c55-5129-baaf-57673050ddbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8505" :question/problem "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0e7e0a0-9388-5346-ab96-f2dec237c9f5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048822}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670698} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526323} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186670691}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"} :step/id #uuid "d0828ae4-bb90-5c40-a13f-3b9425c359b5" :step/math-academy-id 31618 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171845 :question/id #uuid "0444de80-19f1-5251-b159-5dc55756bb19" :question/math-academy-id "e-8506" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd092f29-08bf-5425-88a4-8cba4f0a31c8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048480}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564924} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172648} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564921}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"} :step/id #uuid "ccf338ec-866f-599b-b8a2-46ea4f29e9bb" :step/math-academy-id 31619 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2334101-5266-5853-aedf-04c85746b5cc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048179}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536269} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172640} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172641} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253573} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395465}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"} :step/id #uuid "b07f7df9-365a-5d7b-8326-2325f8154a52" :step/math-academy-id 31620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173161 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the slope field of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}=x-y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation is a function of both $x$ and $y.$ However, the procedure for sketching the slope field is the same as usual.\n\nWe start by setting up a table of values.\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=0$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=-1$ |  |  |  |\n\nWe then calculate the slope at each point and record each answer in the table. For example, the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right|_{(0,1)} = 0-1 ={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$\n\nFilling in our table gives the following set of results:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1 \\\\\n\\hline\n -1\t&0 \t&1 \t&2\\\\\n\\hline\n0\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1\\\\\n\\hline\n1\t&-2 \t&{\\color{blue}{-1}} \t&0\\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ | $-2$ | ${\\color{blue}{-1}}$ | $0$ |\n| $y=0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y=-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, sketching our slope field, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2a/2a86de3bdb5716e2be58f02124ccfd7470c9d8eda50abdb01052200e9fecfa0d.png)\n\nWe're done!\n\nNote that if we were to pick more points and use a computer to plot our slope field, it would look more like this:\n\n![](images/42/42508880923b52e9a63b90514c01b33b68c45385cb1650e44832953a6111ee7b.png)\n\nFrom here, it's easy to get an idea of the solution curves. For example, three particular solutions to the differential equation are given below.\n\n![](images/76/7634837eabf2be5ceb2ac56d22576170238b2640181aadde2417e8ff7a2bbd83.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "790a4898-1668-5214-a911-83977b559c34" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5050 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a89f8367-e99c-56cc-90c3-8399250144a7" :step/math-academy-id 31623 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171847 :question/id #uuid "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8576" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75710e99-e13c-54ba-9401-486bfec15284" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048795}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172649} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362211}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"} :step/id #uuid "1b9a1add-dc4b-5533-80c6-2e82bf931b5c" :step/math-academy-id 31856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173194 :tutorial/content "Note the following facts regarding slope fields:\n\n- Recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x),$ the slope field has *vertical* symmetry.\n\n- Also recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = g(y),$ the slope field has *horizontal* symmetry.\n\n- In contrast, a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x,y)$ usually has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry.\n\nWe can use these facts to determine the type of equation that generates a particular slope field. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "03296d2b-05de-5077-8eab-c619925af706" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5668 :tutorial/title "Symmetry in Slope Fields"} :step/id #uuid "8cc2f789-24f5-5327-b95b-19f422496a28" :step/math-academy-id 34795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905}}] :activity/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172174}]}
